# Nathejk Skan — Project Rules & Context

> Org-wide conventions live in `.agents/rules/rules.md` (Docker, Traefik,
> JetStream, git, task board). Read that too. This file describes **this repo**
> and where it deviates from the org standard.

---

## Overview

**Skan** is the QR scanning service for **Nathejk**, a Danish scouting night race
where patrols (*patruljer*) navigate from checkpoint to checkpoint while being
hunted by *banditter*.

Every patrol carries a paper map with a unique QR sticker on it. Patrols receive
new maps several times during the race, and **every map has its own QR code**.
Two things happen with those codes:

1. **Registration** — the first time a code is scanned, it is unknown to the
   system. The scanner is asked for the patrol's team number (the big number on
   the scouts' arms, *not* the small number next to the QR code), which binds
   that QR code to that patrol for the rest of its life.
2. **Scanning** — every later scan of that code records *who* scanned it, *when*,
   and *where* (browser geolocation, or a hand-typed map coordinate as fallback).

This repo is what a phone lands on when someone scans a sticker: the QR encodes a
URL into this service. An anonymous visitor is asked to log in with their phone
number; the number is matched against event personnel and clan seniors, which is
also how the app knows whether the scanner is a **bandit** or a **crew member** —
the two are meant to get different views.

Skan only *produces* scan facts. Aggregation, reporting and admin live in other
Nathejk services (notably `hq`), which consume the same event stream.

---

## Architecture

Skan is a single Go binary that is both a server-rendered web app and a
CQRS/event-sourcing participant on NATS JetStream.

```
  QR sticker  ──scan──▶  https://skan…/qr/{qrId}/{checksum}
                              │
                         ┌────▼──────────────────────┐  publish   ┌────────────────┐
                         │   Go binary (go/)         │ ─────────▶ │ NATS JetStream │
                         │   html/template pages     │            │   "NATHEJK"    │
                         │   commands/  (write)      │            └───────┬────────┘
                         │   table/     (read)       │◀── MySQL ◀─────────┘ replay
                         └───────────────────────────┘        (projections)
```

- **Write side** — `nathejk/commands/qr.go` publishes three events and never
  writes SQL directly: `qr.*.found`, `qr.*.registered`, `qr.*.scanned`.
- **Read side** — `nathejk/table/*` packages implement `streaminterface.Consumer`
  and materialise events into MariaDB. `xstream.Mux` wires them up; on every
  start the whole log is replayed, so the read model is disposable.
- Skan **consumes projections it does not own**: `patrulje`, `klan`, `senior`,
  `personnel` are populated from events published by other services (mainly
  `hq`/tilmelding). Skan owns only `qr` and `scan`.

### Photographs (`photo`, `photocover`)

`nathejk/table/photo` and `nathejk/table/photocover` are present but **wired to
nothing** — see task 005. They are also the **reference implementation of the
target shape** for projections in this org (see *The library migration* below), so
read them before writing a new one.

- `photo` is a **verbatim copy of the `foto` service's package**, bound for
  `shared-go`. Do not edit it; adapt around it. `photocover` is `hq`'s entity and
  must be constructed here with a **nil publisher** — choosing a cover is not a
  scanner's job.
- They store **content-hash refs, never bytes**, and `photo` never exposes the
  original (it carries the camera's metadata, including GPS). The bytes are served
  by the `foto` service at `<foto-base-url>/photos/<ref>`, e.g.
  `https://foto.local.nathejk.dk/photos/e3875acc…a567`. The base URL **must come
  from an environment variable** — never hardcode a host.

### The library migration

This repo's streaming and projection plumbing is **being replaced**, and the two
photo packages are already written against the new libraries:

| Legacy, in-repo | Replacement |
|---|---|
| `superfluids/` (`streaminterface`, `jetstream`, `xstream`) | `github.com/jrgensen/stream` |
| `pkg/tablerow`, `pkg/sqlpersister`, and more | `github.com/jrgensen/cqrs` |

All projections are destined for `github.com/nathejk/shared-go` once they have
stabilised. That is why `photo` must stay byte-identical to its origin, and why new
work should not deepen the repo's dependence on `superfluids`/`tablerow`.

**Direction of travel:** new projections use `cqrs`/`stream`; existing ones migrate
(task 007). Neither library is in `go.mod` yet, so nothing using them compiles
today.

### Event subjects

Pattern: `NATHEJK.{year}.{entity}.{id}.{event}`

The year comes from the **required `YEAR` environment variable** (dev: `2026`),
threaded through `commands.New`. There is deliberately no default — a wrong year
fails silently, because year-keyed reads simply find nothing. Hardcoded years are
not allowed.

Published by this repo:

| Subject | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `NATHEJK.{year}.qr.{qrId}.found` | An unregistered code was scanned for the first time |
| `NATHEJK.{year}.qr.{qrId}.registered` | A code was bound to a patrol |
| `NATHEJK.{year}.qr.{qrId}.scanned` | A registered code was scanned (with location) |

Note `SubjectFromStr` replaces the **first** `:` with `.`, so `NATHEJK:2025.qr…`
and `NATHEJK.2025.qr…` are the same subject. Both spellings appear in the code;
prefer the `.` form in new code.

### Request flow

| Route | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `GET /` | Manual entry page (type a team number instead of scanning) |
| `POST /` | Records a scan from the manual page |
| `GET /qr/{id}/{cs}` | **Scan landing page.** Unknown code → publishes `found`, redirects to `/map/…`. Known code → renders the confirmation/geolocation page |
| `POST /qr/{id}/{cs}` | Login form target for the scan page |
| `GET /map/{id}/{cs}` | Registration page — asks for / confirms the team number |
| `POST /map/{id}/{cs}` | Publishes `registered`, redirects back to `/qr/…` |
| `PUT /register` | JSON endpoint the scan page calls with geolocation → publishes `scanned` |
| `POST /login`, `GET /logout` | Phone-number login (see below) |
| `GET /qr?n=N` | Generates a CSV of `id,url` for the first N codes — the sticker-printing feed. Token-protected, **not** login-protected |
| `GET /geo` | JSON scan dump with coordinates, for GIS/map export. Token-protected, **not** login-protected |
| `GET /about`, `GET /healthcheck` | Static page / health probe |
| `GET /*` | Static files from `/webroot` |

### QR identity and checksum

QR ids are plain incrementing integers. Each URL carries an adler32 checksum of
`"{qrId}:{SECRET}"` (`main.Checksum`) so codes cannot be enumerated by hand.
Handlers reject a mismatched checksum with `417 Expectation Failed`. **`SECRET`
is a required environment variable** — changing it invalidates every printed
sticker.

### Authentication and roles

There is no external auth service here (unlike `hq`). `internal/login`:

- `POST /login` takes a phone number, looks it up in the `personnel` projection
  and then the `senior` projection, and stores the resulting user as a
  **base64-encoded JSON cookie** named `user`.
- `Authenticate(next, login)` renders the login page instead of the target when
  the cookie is absent.
- The cookie is **not signed, not HttpOnly and not Secure** — it is trivially
  forgeable. Treat this as a known, deliberate-for-now trade-off; do not build
  anything security-sensitive on top of it without raising it first.

**A scanner has exactly one role, and it is derived from the phone number.** The
older PHP app assigned people roles they could switch between per session
(`twig/choose.twig`); that concept is **gone and is not coming back**. Do not
reintroduce role selection, a role switcher, or a role stored in the session.

The intended rules — **decided, not yet implemented**:

| Phone found in | Role |
|---|---|
| `personnel` only | crew |
| `senior` only | bandit |
| **both** | **refuse the login** |
| neither | refuse the login |

**"Senior" and "bandit" are the same thing** — a senior signed up to a klan *is* a
bandit, so presence in the `senior` projection is the whole bandit test. There is
no per-person flag to consult and no subset of seniors that does something else.
(`geoHandler` already relies on this, labelling every senior `"Bandit"`.)

A phone number registered as both crew and senior is a **data error upstream**,
not a case to disambiguate in the UI. Do not guess, and do not prefer one table
over the other: block the login and tell the user (in Danish) to contact HQ so
one of the two registrations can be removed. Note that today's `userByPhone`
does exactly the wrong thing here — it returns on the first `personnel` hit and
never notices the conflict.

