## Project structure

```
skan/
├── docker-compose.yml       # dev stack: api, mysql, adminer, redis, gw
├── docker/Dockerfile        # multistage: base → dev (air) / build → prod
├── webroot/                 # static assets served at /
└── go/                      # module nathejk.dk, Go 1.24 — the whole app
    ├── main.go              # wiring: jetstream, db, projections, mux, commands
    ├── app.go               # config (flags + env), http.Server
    ├── routes.go            # chi router + ALL handlers and templates parsing
    ├── database.go, env.go, server.go
    ├── templates/           # html/template pages (embedded via //go:embed)
    ├── internal/
    │   ├── login/           # phone-number login + cookie auth
    │   ├── data/            # Models struct: the read-side interfaces
    │   ├── logging/, jsonlog/, validator/, vcs/
    ├── nathejk/
    │   ├── commands/        # write side (qr.go is the live one; team.go is inherited)
    │   └── table/           # projections: qr, scan, patrulje, klan, senior, personnel
    │                     #   + photo, photocover — copied in, not yet wired (task 005)
    ├── pkg/sqlpersister/    # LEGACY → github.com/jrgensen/cqrs
    ├── pkg/tablerow/        # LEGACY → github.com/jrgensen/cqrs
    └── superfluids/         # LEGACY → github.com/jrgensen/stream
```

Several files are **inherited from sibling Nathejk repos and unused here** —
`nathejk/table/spejder`, `signup`, `payment`, `pincode*`, `registrant*`,
`nathejk/commands/team.go`, `internal/data/{teams,members,users,tokens,permissions}`,
and `nathejk/table/scanner/` (a verbatim copy of `personnel`, still declaring
`package personnel`). Do not extend them; if you touch that area, prefer deleting
the dead copy over editing it.

### Legacy note

This app was a PHP/Twig application before it was ported to Go. A `go/twig/`
directory held the pre-port originals as an untracked local reference; it has been
**deleted**. Everything worth keeping from it is captured in *What each role may
see* below. Do not recreate it.

---

## What each role may see

**Rule of thumb: bandits may only learn about bandits. It has to be a fair game.**

A bandit is a player. Anything a bandit sees about a patrol's progress through the
race is an unfair advantage, so a bandit's view is restricted to information their
own side produced: their own catches, and other bandits' catches of that patrol.
Never show a bandit checkpoint activity, total scan counts, positions from crew
scans, or anything derived from them.

Crew are not players. A crew member may be checkpoint personnel, a guide helping
the scouts, or a samarit patching up blisters — all of them legitimately need the
full picture. **Crew may see everything.**

| | Bandit | Crew |
|---|---|---|
| The patrol's identity, name, photo | yes | yes |
| `catchCount` — times caught by bandits | yes | yes |
| Total scan count (all scanners) | **no** | yes |
| Checkpoint / crew scan activity, positions | **no** | yes |
| Free-text remark about the patrol | yes | yes |

Definitions:

- **`catchCount`** — how many times the patrol has been **caught by bandits**,
  i.e. the number of scans of that patrol whose scanner is a senior. This is the
  one count a bandit is allowed to know.
- **scan count** — total scans by anyone, crew included. Crew-only.

When adding anything to the scan-result page, decide which column of that table it
belongs in *before* writing the handler. Default to crew-only if unsure.

### Counting catches: the rescan rule

A rescan **counts**. Bandits legitimately catch the same patrol more than once,
so `catchCount` is a plain count of scans, not of distinct scanners.

But accidental double scans must not inflate it. The guard — **decided, not yet
implemented**:

> Look at the patrol's **single most recent scan**. If it was made by the *same*
> scanner *less than 30 minutes* ago, ask for an explicit confirmation
> ("skal dette tælle som en ny scanning?") before recording. Otherwise record it
> straight away.

Consequences of that phrasing, all intended:

| Situation | Result |
|---|---|
| Same scanner, same patrol, 5 min apart, nothing in between | confirmation required |
| Same scanner, same patrol, 45 min apart | recorded, no question |
| Same scanner twice, but another scanner scanned in between | recorded, no question |
| A different scanner scans right after | recorded, no question |

Rules for implementing it:

- The check is **server-side**, on the patrol's scan history — not on the browser,
  and not on the QR code (a patrol may carry a new map with a new code).
- A confirmation must never *drop* the scan silently. Unconfirmed means "not
  recorded yet, ask the user", and the user must be able to say yes and have it
  count.
- The existing flow is `GET /qr/{id}/{cs}` → page → `PUT /register` with the
  position. So the natural shape is for `PUT /register` to answer "this needs
  confirming" and for the page to re-`PUT` with an explicit confirm flag. Do not
  move the decision into the template.
- This needs a new read on the `scan` projection (latest scan for a team); today's
  querier only has `GetAll` and a broken `GetByID`.

### Protecting `/qr` and `/geo`

These two are machine endpoints, not scanner pages: `/qr` feeds the sticker
printing and `/geo` feeds map/GIS export. They are **not** behind the phone login
— they are protected by a **secret token passed as a GET parameter**, checked
before anything else runs.

- Use a **separate environment variable** for it, *not* `SECRET`. `SECRET` seeds
  the QR checksum; a token travels in URLs, browser history, proxy logs and
  `Referer` headers, and leaking `SECRET` would let anyone mint valid sticker URLs
  for every code. Keep the two unrelated.
- Compare with `crypto/subtle.ConstantTimeCompare`, and refuse when the token is
  empty or the env var is unset — never fail open.
- Respond `404` to a bad or missing token, so the endpoint's existence isn't
  advertised.
- These endpoints stay crew-grade information. Do not hand the token to bandits;
  `/geo` is a live map of the whole race and would break the fair-game rule
  outright.

**Neither is protected today** — both are wired without `user.Authenticate` and
without a token check in `routes.go`, so they are fully public. Do not add new
endpoints to that unprotected set.

---

## Technology stack

- **Go 1.24**, module `nathejk.dk`, single binary, no separate `cmd/` dir
- **Router**: `go-chi/chi/v5`
- **Views**: `html/template`, parsed per request from an `embed.FS`, each page
  composed as `base.html` + one page template
- **Client-side**: no build step. Bootstrap 3 + jQuery from CDN, plus inline
  `<script>` for geolocation and the `PUT /register` fetch
- **DB**: MariaDB 10.8, `go-sql-driver/mysql`, hand-written SQL
- **Events**: NATS JetStream. Currently via the in-repo `superfluids/jetstream`;
  migrating to `github.com/jrgensen/stream` + `github.com/jrgensen/cqrs` (task 007)
- **Shared domain types/messages**: `github.com/nathejk/shared-go` — also where
  projections go once they stabilise
- **Dev loop**: `air` (`go/go.air.toml`) as a Go tool — rebuilds and reruns tests
  on change
- **Quality gates in the prod build**: `go test`, `staticcheck`, `govulncheck`
  all run in the `build` stage, so a lint or vuln failure fails the image

`redis` / `REDIS_ADDR` are provisioned in compose but not used by the Go code.

---

## Development

```sh
docker compose up          # api + mysql + adminer + gw
```

Requires the external `traefik` and `jetstream` networks from the org infra repo.
Everything runs in containers — never run `go` on the host. One-offs:
`docker compose run --rm api go test ./...`.

| Service | URL |
|---|---|
| App | `https://skan.local.nathejk.dk` |
| Adminer | `http://mysql.skan.local.nathejk.dk` |
| Redis Commander | `http://redis.skan.local.nathejk.dk` |

### Environment

| Variable | Dev value | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `YEAR` | `2026` | event year slug. **Required, no default** — an unset value stops the process at boot. Overridable with `-year` |
| `JETSTREAM_DSN` | `nats://dev.nathejk.dk:4222` | points at **shared dev**, not the local `jetstream` network |
| `DB_DSN` | `root:ib@tcp(mysql:3306)/skan?parseTime=true` | |
| `SECRET` | `nh2025` | seeds the QR checksum; required. **Never** put this in a URL |
| *token var for `/qr` + `/geo`* | not yet added | separate secret, passed as a GET parameter — see *Protecting `/qr` and `/geo`* |
| *foto base URL* | not yet added | e.g. `https://foto.local.nathejk.dk`; photos are at `<base>/photos/<ref>`. Required by tasks 005/006 — never hardcode the host |
| `WEBROOT` | `/www` (flag default) | compose mounts `/webroot`; `routes.go` hardcodes `/webroot` |
| `SMS_DSN`, `REDIS_ADDR` | set | currently unused by the code |

Tables are created on boot with `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS`, so **adding a
column to a `table.sql` does nothing where the table already exists** — drop the
table (or the DB) in dev.

---

## Known deviations & rough edges

Flag or fix these deliberately; don't quietly copy the pattern.

- **Compose uses a `gw` gateway container** (`jrgensen/gateway`) with
  `PROXY_MAPPINGS` and a single Traefik router, instead of the org's per-service
  Traefik labels. `api` also publishes a port. Migrating to the standard pattern
  is a pending piece of work.
- **`JETSTREAM_DSN` points at `dev.nathejk.dk`**, so local runs read and write the
  shared dev stream.
- **Bandit vs crew views are not implemented.** `scanHandler` hardcodes
  `isBandit: true`, `scanCount: 10`, `catchCount: 1`. The role should come from
  the phone-number lookup, and the two views must differ per *What each role may
  see* — today a crew member gets the restricted bandit page, and the counts are
  fiction either way.
- **`/geo` and `/qr` are completely public** — no login, no token — which breaks
  the fair-game rule outright. See *Protecting `/qr` and `/geo`*.
- **Rescans are unguarded.** Any repeat scan counts immediately; the 30-minute
  same-scanner confirmation is not implemented. Note also that the `scan` table's
  primary key is `(qrId, uts)` with `INSERT IGNORE`, so two scans of one code in
  the *same second* are silently discarded — that is accidental, not the rule.
- **An unrecognised phone number produces a 500, not a message.** `LoginHandler`
  marshals a `nil` user into the cookie as `null`; the next request then fails
  `UserFromRequest` with a non-`ErrNoCookie` error, and `Authenticate` turns that
  into `500 server error`. Failed and refused logins both need to re-render the
  login page with a Danish explanation instead.
- `coordinates.html` references `.remark`, which no handler supplies — comparing
  a missing map key with `""` is a template execution error. It is a free-text
  note about the patrol, carried over from the PHP app's `noticeText`.
- `doIndexHandler` (`POST /`) publishes a scan with the literal QR id `"x"`.
- The `prod` Dockerfile stage copies the binary but **not** `webroot/`, while
  `routes.go` serves `/webroot` — static files are missing in production images.
- The year slug used by the projections' `year` **column** is a separate concern
  from `YEAR`: a projection must take it from the message subject, so a replay of an
  older year is not relabelled with the current one. `scan` has the column but never
  fills it, so `scan.Filter{YearSlug: …}` only works via its empty-string bypass;
  `qr` has no `year` column at all.
- **`go build ./...` and `go test ./...` currently fail**: the copied `photo`/
  `photocover` packages import `github.com/jrgensen/cqrs`, which is not in `go.mod`.
  This breaks the `air` dev loop (it runs the tests before rebuilding) and the
  production image (the `build` stage runs `go test`, `staticcheck`, `govulncheck`).
  Task 005 fixes it by adding the dependency.
- Consumers `log.Fatalf` on a SQL error, i.e. a bad row kills the process.
- `go.mod` lists `go-chi/chi/v5` as `// indirect` although it is a direct
  dependency; `go mod tidy` would fix it.

